menu "USB Audio (subsys/usb_audio)"

    config USB_AUDIO_FEED_PERIOD_US
        int "Feeder period (us)"
        default 1000
        range 250 4000
        help
            How often the feeder task moves bytes from the ring into TinyUSB's
            software FIFO while streaming. 1000 us matches the USB frame. Shorter
            lowers latency at the cost of wakeups; longer relies on the FIFO
            (CONFIG_TINYUSB_AUDIO_EP_IN_SW_BUF_MULT) to bridge the gap.

    config USB_AUDIO_FEED_TASK_PRIORITY
        int "Feeder task priority"
        default 19
        range 1 24
        help
            Between audio capture (20) and the USB device task (18): the feeder must
            run as soon as capture publishes, but never delay capture itself.

    config USB_AUDIO_FEED_TASK_CORE
        int "Feeder task core affinity"
        default 1
        range 0 1
        help
            Same core as capture and USB so the whole audio path is isolated from
            the radio stacks on core 0 (ADR-009).

    config USB_AUDIO_FEED_TASK_STACK
        int "Feeder task stack (bytes)"
        default 3072
        range 2048 8192

    config USB_AUDIO_SPEAKER_TARGET_MS
        int "Speaker: backlog the feedback servo holds in the playback ring (ms)"
        default 12
        range 2 200
        depends on TINYUSB_AUDIO_SPEAKER_ENABLED
        help
            The setpoint the asynchronous-sink servo regulates (ADR-021). It is the
            latency the USB path adds on top of the playback driver's own prefill,
            so keep it small; but it must stay above that prefill, or the two
            controllers fight - audio_playback re-enters prefill whenever the ring
            runs dry, which the servo then reads as "send faster".

            Rule of thumb: CONFIG_AUDIO_PLAYBACK_PREFILL_MS plus a couple of
            milliseconds of host jitter.

    config USB_AUDIO_SPEAKER_FB_PERIOD_MS
        int "Speaker: feedback recomputation period (ms)"
        default 16
        range 1 128
        depends on TINYUSB_AUDIO_SPEAKER_ENABLED
        help
            The backlog is sampled every feeder tick and averaged over this period
            before a new feedback value is sent. Averaging is what keeps host jitter
            out of the servo; too short and the value chatters, too long and genuine
            drift is corrected slowly. The endpoint keeps sending the last value
            every frame regardless, so this is the servo's period, not the wire's.

endmenu
